\section{Why the cause column has to be coded openly}{

\code{"Alive or dead of other cause"} is carried by living subjects as well as
by those who died of something else. It therefore cannot be passed as
\code{cause_other_value}: \code{\link[=prep_surv]{prep_surv()}} treats a declared cause of death on a
living subject as a contradiction and stops, which is the behaviour you
want for every other value. Leave \code{cause_other_value} at its default
\code{NULL} instead. An undeclared value on a dead subject then becomes
\code{event_cr = 2}, and the same value on a living subject is simply ignored,
because vital status has already settled the case.

The cost of open coding is that a value nobody anticipated -- a new
registry code, a typo -- also becomes a death from other causes rather than
an error. Check \code{attr(d, "os_cr_table")} and the counts in
\code{attr(d, "prep_surv")} against your own extract before going further.
